.TH "tangsible-ee" "1" "2026-11-19" "Tangsible" "Tangsible Manual"
.PP
.SH NAME
.PP
tangsible-ee - draft an execution-environment.\&yml from a playbook, check it, build it, or push it
.PP
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\fBtangsible ee\fR <init | verify | build | push> [\fIplaybook.\&yml\fR] [\fIoptions\fR .\&.\&.\&]
.PP
\fBtangsible ee init\fR [\fIplaybook.\&yml\fR] [--base \fIimage\fR] [--resolve-packages] [--no-backup]
.br
\fBtangsible ee verify\fR [\fIplaybook.\&yml\fR] [-f \fIfile\fR] [--keep] [--no-cache] [--show-output] [\fIansible-playbook args\fR .\&.\&.\&]
.br
\fBtangsible ee build\fR [-f \fIfile\fR] [-t \fItag\fR .\&.\&.\&] [--no-cache] [--show-output]
.br
\fBtangsible ee push\fR [-f \fIfile\fR] [-y | --yes]
.PP
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\fBee init\fR drafts an execution-environment.\&yml; \fBee verify\fR builds one into a
throw-away image and checks it (see \fBVERIFY\fR below); \fBee build\fR builds it into
the image it names and keeps it (see \fBBUILD\fR below); \fBee push\fR uploads the
image names a definition lists to their registry (see \fBPUSH\fR below).\&
.PP
.SS INIT
.PP
\fBee init\fR writes a draft \fBexecution-environment.\&yml\fR (ansible-builder schema
version 3) to the current directory.\& The stock execution environment images
carry almost no collections, so using one for real work means building a
custom image, and most of that definition is boilerplate.\& The draft fills in
what can be determined; everything else is marked with a \fB# TODO\fR comment.\&
It is a starting point to review and extend, not a complete definition, and
\fBtangsible\fR does not build the image (use \fBansible-builder\fR(1) for that).\&
.PP
If \fIplaybook.\&yml\fR is omitted, it is resolved the same way as
\fBtangsible-run\fR(1) (see \fBtangsible\fR(1)).\&
.PP
It does the following:
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
Reads the playbook and the roles it uses (\fBroles:\fR, \fBinclude_role\fR,
\fBimport_role\fR, role dependencies), and collects the module of every task,
including handlers, \fBblock\fR/\fBrescue\fR/\fBalways\fR and the \fBaction:\fR and
\fBlocal_action:\fR forms.\& Modules only: filters, lookups and tests are not
analyzed.\&
.IP \(bu 4
Asks the host'\&s Ansible (\fBansible-doc\fR(1)) which collection owns each
module, honoring \fBcollections:\fR keywords.\& \fBansible.\&builtin\fR modules are
left out, as ansible-core ships with them.\& A module the host has no
collection for, a templated module name and a templated \fBinclude_tasks\fR or
\fBinclude_role\fR target become \fB# TODO\fR comments.\&
.IP \(bu 4
Takes collections and roles from a \fBrequirements.\&yml\fR (or \fBcollections/requirements.\&yml\fR)
next to the playbook or in the current directory, if there is one, and adds
the collections the playbook uses that it doesn'\&t list.\& Its versions win;
otherwise a collection is pinned to the version installed on the host
(\fBansible-galaxy collection list\fR), or left unpinned with a comment.\&
.IP \(bu 4
Pulls the base image if it is missing (using \fBpull_policy\fR) and inspects it
with \fBpodman\fR for its package manager, Python and ansible-core.\&
.IP \(bu 4
Annotates each collection with where it was found.\&
.IP \(bu 4
Suggests the name of the built image as a commented \fBtags\fR stanza
(the project directory'\&s name plus \fB-ee:latest\fR) to uncomment and adjust.\&
.IP \(bu 4
Finds the lookup plugins the playbook uses (\fBlookup()\fR, \fBquery()\fR, \fBq()\fR with a
literal name, and \fBwith_*\fR-loops, in task files, variable files,
\fBgroup_vars\fR and Jinja templates under the playbook'\&s directory); their
collections are added, with the plugin'\&s documented requirements as a
comment.\& A name that is not a literal cannot be determined and becomes a
single \fB# TODO\fR.\& Filters are not analyzed.\&
.IP \(bu 4
Offers packages that used lookups and modules need and their collections
don'\&t declare (for example \fBdnspython\fR for the \fBdig\fR lookup, \fBrsync\fR for
\fBsynchronize\fR), from a small built-in table, as a commented \fBpython:\fR and
\fBsystem:\fR block to uncomment.\&
.IP \(bu 4
Lists the binaries that \fBcommand\fR and \fBshell\fR tasks running on the
controller (and so inside the execution environment) use and the base image
lacks, as a comment block.\& Only tasks detected as running on the controller
count: a play with \fBhosts: localhost\fR (or \fB127.\&0.\&0.\&1\fR, \fB::1\fR) or
\fBconnection: local\fR, or a task or block with \fBconnection: local\fR,
\fBdelegate_to: localhost\fR, \fBansible_connection: local\fR in its \fBvars\fR, or
\fBlocal_action\fR.\& Tasks on other hosts are the targets'\& business.\& A role'\&s
task file is looked at without its play, so only the task-level conditions
apply to it.\& The binary is the first word of each command of a shell line
(builtins, shell keywords, assignments and wrappers like \fBsudo\fR are skipped);
one that is templated cannot be determined and becomes a single \fB# TODO\fR.\&
.PD
.PP
The base image is, in this order: \fB--base\fR \fIimage\fR, \fBbase_image\fR under
\fB[execution_environment]\fR in \fBconfig.\&toml\fR, or the built-in default
(a pinned \fBghcr.\&io/ansible-community/community-ee-base\fR tag, named in the
draft'\&s header when used).\& \fBimage\fR under \fB[execution_environment]\fR is never
used: it is the image tangsible runs, often the result of building this very
draft.\&
.PP
\fB--resolve-packages\fR
.RS 4
Also look up which package provides each missing binary, and offer them as
a \fBsystem:\fR block to uncomment (bindep syntax, with \fB[platform:rpm]\fR or
\fB[platform:dpkg]\fR).\& This starts a throw-away container of the base image
(the image itself is never changed) with network access, as root, and
downloads repository metadata, so it can take from half a minute to a
couple of minutes: \fBdnf\fR is queried on Fedora/RHEL-family images (the image
needs \fBdnf\fR; \fBmicrodnf\fR can'\&t answer this) and \fBapt-file\fR on
Debian/Ubuntu-family images.\& A binary with several candidate packages is
listed with all of them, one with none as a \fB# TODO\fR.\& If the lookup can'\&t be
done (no network, no \fBdnf\fR), the binaries are only listed.\& The
\fBnetwork\fR key under \fB[execution_environment]\fR is used for the container
(\fBhost\fR fixes DNS trouble).\& Nothing is ever added without you uncommenting it.\&
.PP
.RE
If \fBexecution-environment.\&yml\fR already exists it is replaced.\& When the new draft
differs, the old file is first kept as \fBexecution-environment.\&yml.\&\fR\fIN\fR, where \fIN\fR
is the highest existing number plus one (1 for the first); only the five
highest-numbered backups are kept, the lowest are removed.\& When the content is
identical nothing is written and no backup is made.\& The new draft is generated
from scratch: your edits are in the backup, not merged.\& The file is replaced
atomically (a temporary file is written, the backup is a hard link, then a
rename), so an interrupted run never leaves a missing or half-written file.\& A
symlink or a directory at that name is an error.\&
.PP
\fB--no-backup\fR
.RS 4
Replace an existing file without keeping its old content; existing
backups are left alone and no backup is made or removed.\&
.PP
.RE
.SH VERIFY
.PP
\fBee verify\fR builds an \fBexecution-environment.\&yml\fR (\fIfile\fR, default
\fB.\&/execution-environment.\&yml\fR, normally the draft after you have edited it) into
a throw-away image and checks that image against what the playbook uses.\& It
needs \fBpodman\fR and \fBansible-builder\fR (\fBtangsible version\fR shows both).\& If
\fIplaybook.\&yml\fR is omitted it is resolved like \fBtangsible-run\fR(1); other
arguments, such as \fB-i\fR or \fB-e\fR, are passed to \fBansible-playbook\fR for the
syntax check.\& \fB--ask-pass\fR, \fB--ask-become-pass\fR and \fB--ask-vault-pass\fR are not
supported: a syntax check needs no secrets, use a vault password file.\&
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
It builds the image with \fBansible-builder build\fR under a private tag
(\fBlocalhost/tangsible-ee-verify:.\&.\&.\&\fR), which overrides the \fBtags\fR of the
definition, so an image you named is never created or overwritten.\& The
\fBnetwork\fR key under \fB[execution_environment]\fR is used for the build too
(\fBhost\fR fixes DNS trouble).\& A build takes minutes; its output goes to
\fB.\&tangsible/ee-verify.\&log\fR, and a failed build shows the end of it.\&
.IP \(bu 4
In the built image it runs \fBansible-playbook --syntax-check\fR, the way a
real \fBtangsible run\fR would run it (the same mounts and environment).\&
.IP \(bu 4
It reports the image'\&s ansible-core version next to the host'\&s, as
information only (a run that behaves differently in the container often
differs in the version).\&
.IP \(bu 4
In one more run it asks the image'\&s \fBansible-doc\fR about \fBevery\fR module and
lookup the playbook uses (so all missing ones are listed, not only the first
one the syntax check stops at) and checks that the binaries used by
\fBcommand\fR/\fBshell\fR tasks that run on the controller exist.\&
.IP \(bu 4
It removes the image and the build context, unless \fB--keep\fR is given, which
leaves the image and prints its name.\& podman'\&s build cache stays (see
\fB--no-cache\fR).\& A build that fails, or is interrupted
with Ctrl-C, can leave working containers and a temporary image behind; those
are removed too (only what the build created: nothing that was there before is
touched, so do not run another podman build at the same time).\&
.PD
.PP
This catches missing collections, a broken build, missing ansible-core or
ansible-runner and missing binaries.\& It does not catch Python libraries that a
plugin imports only when it runs, or anything a syntax check does not execute.\&
.PP
\fB-f\fR \fIfile\fR, \fB--file\fR \fIfile\fR
.RS 4
The definition to build, instead of \fBexecution-environment.\&yml\fR.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB--keep\fR
.RS 4
Keep the built image and print its name.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB--show-output\fR
.RS 4
Stream the build'\&s complete output to the terminal (stderr) instead of
the status line.\& The status line, shown while the build runs when stderr is
a terminal, has the elapsed time, podman'\&s latest \fB[stage/stages] STEP
step/steps\fR marker and the latest line of output; it is erased when the
build ends.\& There is no percentage: podman does not tell how many steps a
stage has until it gets there.\& The full output always goes to the log.\&
(\fB-v\fR and \fB--verbose\fR are not used for this: \fBee verify\fR passes them to
\fBansible-playbook\fR.\&)
.PP
.RE
\fB--no-cache\fR
.RS 4
Build without podman'\&s build cache.\& By default the cache is used and kept
(untagged stage images, which make a second build faster but take disk
space; \fBpodman image prune\fR removes them), so a layer that installs
unpinned packages is not re-run.\& Use this to confirm the definition builds
from scratch; nothing is left behind.\&
.PP
.RE
.SH BUILD
.PP
\fBee build\fR builds an \fBexecution-environment.\&yml\fR (\fIfile\fR, default
\fB.\&/execution-environment.\&yml\fR) with \fBansible-builder build\fR and keeps the image.\&
It needs \fBpodman\fR and \fBansible-builder\fR, takes no playbook and runs no checks
(\fBee verify\fR does).\& The image name is \fB-t\fR \fItag\fR, else the definition'\&s
\fBoptions.\&tags\fR; with neither it stops before building (\fBansible-builder\fR
would otherwise create \fBansible-execution-env:latest\fR; \fBee init\fR'\&s draft has a
commented \fBtags\fR stanza).\& The build uses a private temporary context, so no
\fBcontext/\fR directory appears in the project; the \fBnetwork\fR key under
\fB[execution_environment]\fR is used for it (\fBhost\fR fixes DNS trouble); its output
goes to \fB.\&tangsible/ee-build.\&log\fR, and a failed build shows the end of it.\&
.PP
On success it prints each tag with the image id and size, notes a tag that
moved off an older image (\fBpodman image prune\fR removes the untagged one) and
prints the \fBimage = ".\&.\&.\&"\fR line to use it, which is never written to
\fBconfig.\&toml\fR.\& A build that fails or is interrupted with Ctrl-C leaves
nothing behind except podman'\&s build cache; an existing tag is untouched.\&
.PP
\fB-f\fR \fIfile\fR, \fB--file\fR \fIfile\fR
.RS 4
The definition to build, instead of \fBexecution-environment.\&yml\fR.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB-t\fR \fItag\fR, \fB--tag\fR \fItag\fR
.RS 4
The image name; repeatable (one build, several names).\& Replaces the
definition'\&s \fBoptions.\&tags\fR.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB--show-output\fR
.RS 4
Stream the build'\&s complete output instead of the status line, as for
\fBee verify\fR.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB--no-cache\fR
.RS 4
Build without podman'\&s build cache, as for \fBee verify\fR.\&
.PP
.RE
.SH PUSH
.PP
\fBee push\fR uploads the image names listed in the definition'\&s \fBoptions.\&tags\fR
(\fIfile\fR, default \fB.\&/execution-environment.\&yml\fR) with \fBpodman push\fR: one push per
name, in order.\& It needs \fBpodman\fR only.\& An image is pushed by name, and the
registry is in the name (\fBregistry.\&example.\&com/team/my-ee:1.\&0\fR); one image can
carry several names (\fB:1.\&0\fR, \fB:latest\fR), the layers go up once.\& A name with no
registry (\fBstd-ee:latest\fR) is local-only: podman keeps it as \fBlocalhost/.\&.\&.\&\fR,
which cannot be pushed, so \fBee push\fR leaves it out and lists it as not pushed.\&
It is an error when no name has a registry.\&
.PP
Before pushing anything it checks the other names (not \fBlocalhost/\fR, the image
present here) and lists all problems at once.\& It then prints what it will push,
grouped by registry with each image'\&s id and size, and the local-only names it
leaves out, and asks \fBProceed?\& [y/N]\fR;
only \fBy\fR or \fByes\fR goes on.\& Without a terminal it stops unless \fB--yes\fR is given.\&
It stops at the first failed push and says which names were already pushed.\&
.PP
Credentials are podman'\&s: log in once with \fBpodman login\fR \fIregistry\fR and
\fBpodman push\fR finds the login (\fBREGISTRY_AUTH_FILE\fR, \fBauth.\&json\fR, credential
helpers); nothing is read or stored by \fBtangsible\fR, and nothing goes into
\fBconfig.\&toml\fR.\& A CI job can set \fBREGISTRY_AUTH_FILE\fR or pipe the secret into
\fBpodman login --password-stdin\fR.\& By default \fBpodman login\fR keeps its token
under \fB$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR\fR, which is cleared at reboot; giving it \fB--authfile\fR
and a file such as \fB~/.\&config/containers/auth.\&json\fR keeps it.\& When a push fails
for lack of a login, \fBtangsible\fR adds the line
\fBnot logged in?\& run: podman login\fR \fIregistry\fR.\&
.PP
\fB-f\fR \fIfile\fR, \fB--file\fR \fIfile\fR
.RS 4
The definition whose names are pushed, instead of
\fBexecution-environment.\&yml\fR.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB-y\fR, \fB--yes\fR
.RS 4
Do not ask for confirmation.\&
.PP
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.PP
\fB0\fR
.RS 4
\fBee init\fR: the draft was written.\& \fBee verify\fR: the build and every check
passed.\& \fBee build\fR: the image was built.\& \fBee push\fR: every name was pushed.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB1\fR
.RS 4
\fBee init\fR: no draft was written (the file is a symlink or a directory, \fBpodman\fR is
missing, the base image couldn'\&t be pulled or inspected, or the playbook
couldn'\&t be read).\& \fBee verify\fR: the build or a check failed, or it
couldn'\&t start (a missing tool or file).\&
\fBee build\fR: the build failed, or it couldn'\&t start (a missing tool or file, or
no image name).\& \fBee push\fR: a name or image is unfit to push, the answer was no,
there was no terminal to ask on and no \fB--yes\fR, or a push failed.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB2\fR
.RS 4
Usage error.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB130\fR
.RS 4
\fBee verify\fR, \fBee build\fR or \fBee push\fR was interrupted.\&
.PP
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
\fBtangsible\fR(1), \fBtangsible-run\fR(1), \fBansible-builder\fR(1), \fBpodman\fR(1), \fBpodman-push\fR(1), \fBpodman-login\fR(1)
